  "num_atoms": 14,
  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Urbain Le Verrier reported in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advanced faster than Newtonian theory predicted.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Mercury's perihelion advances by about 43 arcseconds per century beyond the Newtonian prediction.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Le Verrier had earlier used orbital anomalies of Uranus to predict the existence of Neptune.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Le Verrier proposed that an unseen intra-Mercurial planet or ring of matter caused the excess precession.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "The excess precession arises from unmodelled mass lying inside Mercury's orbit.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "The excess precession arises from a deficiency in Newton's law of gravitation.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "Edmond Lescarbault's report of a transiting dark object was made in 1878.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Repeated eclipse searches failed to yield any confirmable sighting of an intra-Mercurial planet.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "No intra-Mercurial planet has ever been confirmed to exist.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "A planet orbiting inside Mercury's orbit was confirmed by later telescopic observation.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "A body massive enough to produce the observed precession would also have perturbed the node and inclination of Mercury's orbit detectably.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Newcomb's analysis of the planetary tables concluded that the perihelion residual was genuine and not a product of computational error in the planetary perturbations.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "The entire anomaly was an artifact of arithmetic errors in Le Verrier's tables of Venus.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Albert Einstein derived a perihelion advance of 43 arcseconds per century from general relativity in November 1915.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
  ],
